 ## Overview
-This method renders volumetric clouds by ray-marching a procedural noise field within a camera-space bounding box on the GPU, using a fragment shader to compute per-pixel color and alpha [S1, unofficial]. It differs from texture-based cloud rendering by generating density entirely from 3D noise functions (specifically Worley noise combined with Fractal Brownian Motion) rather than sampling pre-baked textures, allowing for infinite resolution and dynamic wind animation without texture streaming [S1, unofficial]. It builds on [Temporal and Procedural Techniques](9442-temporal-and-procedural-techniques.md) by implementing the procedural density generation and temporal animation components directly in the rendering pipeline.
+This method renders volumetric clouds by evaluating a procedural noise field directly within a GPU fragment shader during a direct volume ray casting pass [S1, unofficial]. It replaces texture-based density lookups with on-the-fly noise generation (specifically Worley noise via Fractal Brownian Motion) and approximates light scattering using the Henyey-Greenstein phase function [S1, unofficial]. It is distinguished from texture-based cloud rendering by its ability to generate infinite detail and seamless animation without external asset dependencies, though it incurs higher computational cost per sample due to the noise evaluation and secondary light-marching passes [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs and Outputs:**
-*   **Inputs:** Camera world position, normalized ray direction (derived from screen UVs and camera projection), cloud volume bounding box ($\mathbf{B}_{min}, \mathbf{B}_{max}$), sun direction ($\mathbf{S}$), sun color ($\mathbf{C}_{sun}$), absorption coefficient ($\alpha$), scattering factor ($g$), cloud scale ($\sigma$), octave count ($N$), wind speed (time offset), and a 3D noise texture ($\mathcal{N}$) containing Worley noise values [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Outputs:** Final pixel color ($\mathbf{C}_{final}$) and alpha ($\alpha_{final}$) [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Input:** Camera world position, ray direction (normalized), scene background color (from `_MainTex`), cloud volume bounding box (`_CloudVolumeMinBound`, `_CloudVolumeMaxBound`), sun direction (`_SunDirection`), and shader parameters (`_AbsorptionCoefficient`, `_ScatterFactor`, `_CloudScale`, `_OctaveCount`, `_WindSpeed`) [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Output:** Final pixel color (cloud color composited over background) and alpha [S1, unofficial].
 
 **Data Structures:**
-*   **Bounding Box:** Defined by two 3D vectors $\mathbf{B}_{min}$ and $\mathbf{B}_{max}$ representing the world-space corners of the cloud volume [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Noise Texture:** A 3D texture $\mathcal{N}$ storing scalar density values. The implementation uses Worley noise, sampled at world-scaled coordinates [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Bounding Box:** Defined by two 3D vectors, `_CloudVolumeMinBound` and `_CloudVolumeMaxBound` [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Noise Texture:** A 3D texture (`_NoiseTex`) containing pre-computed Worley noise values, sampled at scaled world coordinates [S1, unofficial]. *Note: While the source code samples a texture, the method is described as "Procedural" because the noise generation logic (FBM) is implemented in the shader, and the texture serves as a base noise source or cache. The density is constructed via FBM in the shader loop.* [S1, unofficial].
 
 **Parameters:**
-*   `_AbsorptionCoefficient` ($\alpha$): Controls light absorption. Higher values darken clouds [S1, unofficial].
-*   `_ScatterFactor` ($g$): Controls forward scattering intensity (Henyey-Greenstein). Higher values increase silver linings [S1, unofficial].
+*   `_AbsorptionCoefficient`: Controls light absorption. Higher values darken clouds [S1, unofficial].
+*   `_ScatterFactor`: Controls forward scattering strength (silver lining effect). Higher values increase forward scattering [S1, unofficial].
 *   `Density Threshold`: Minimum noise value to contribute to density. Higher values create gaps [S1, unofficial].
-*   `Cloud Scale` ($\sigma$): World-space scaling factor for noise coordinates. Higher values create smaller features [S1, unofficial].
-*   `Octave Count` ($N$): Number of FBM layers. Higher values increase edge detail [S1, unofficial].
-*   `Wind Speed`: Time-based offset for noise sampling coordinates [S1, unofficial].
+*   `_CloudScale`: Controls feature size. Higher values result in smaller clouds [S1, unofficial].
+*   `_OctaveCount`: Number of FBM layers. Higher values increase edge detail [S1, unofficial].
+*   `Wind Speed`: Offsets noise coordinates for animation [S1, unofficial].
 
